answer.worked:
At an intersection:
$$\begin{align*}
\cos x &= \frac{1}{2}\sec x \\
2\cos x &= \sec x \\
2\cos x &= \frac{1}{\cos x} \\
2\cos^2 x &= 1 \\
\cos^2 x &= \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}$$
In the first quadrant, $\cos x=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$, so:
$$\begin{align*}
x &= \frac{\pi}{4}
\end{align*}$$

